-1.2 For rationals $0\le u<v\le1$, the usual buy-at-$u$, sell-at-$v$ strategy applied to this bounded martingale has expected gain zero; each completed upcrossing earns at least $v-u$, and an unfinished final holding loses at most one. Therefore the expected number of completed upcrossings by time $N$ is at most $(v-u)^{-1}$. Monotone convergence of the integer counts makes the total upcrossings finite almost surely. Intersect over the countably many rational pairs: each $p_k(a)$ then converges to some $p_\infty(a)\in[0,1]$. [F2, algebra]
+1.2 For rationals $0\le u<v\le1$, the buy-at-$u$, sell-at-$v$ strategy applied to this bounded martingale has expected gain zero; each completed upcrossing earns at least $v-u$, and an unfinished final holding loses at most one. Therefore the expected number of completed upcrossings by time $N$ is at most $(v-u)^{-1}$. By [F7], the total upcrossing count has finite expectation and is finite almost surely. Intersect over the countably many rational pairs and the finite alphabet: each $p_k(a)$ then converges to some $p_\infty(a)\in[0,1]$. [F7, given, algebra]
 
-1.3 For $t\ge0$, let $\tau$ be the first $k$ with $p_k(a)<2^{-t}$. On each event $\{\tau=k\}\in\mathcal F_k$, the tower identity gives $$P(X_0=a,\tau=k)=E[1_{\{\tau=k\}}p_k(a)]\le2^{-t}P(\tau=k).$$ Summing $k$ and then $a$ proves $P(\sup_k g_k>t)\le |\mathcal A|2^{-t}$. Thus $G=\sup_k g_k$ is integrable, and dominated convergence gives $E g_k\to E g_\infty$. [F2, given, algebra]
+1.3 For every finite-past cylinder $B\in\mathcal F_m$, the tower identity gives $\int_Bp_k(a)\,dP=P(B\cap\{X_0=a\})$ for all $k\ge m$. Since $0\le p_k(a)\le1$ and $p_k(a)\to p_\infty(a)$ almost surely, [F6] gives the same identity with $p_\infty(a)$. For fixed $a$, the finite measures $B\mapsto\int_Bp_\infty(a)\,dP$ and $B\mapsto P(B\cap\{X_0=a\})$ agree on the algebra of finite-past cylinders. Their agreement class is a monotone class by continuity of finite measures, so [F5] extends the identity to every $B\in\mathcal F^-$. Thus $p_\infty(a)$ is a conditional probability of $X_0=a$ given the full past. [F5, F6, step 1.2, given]
 
